## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical API-testing skill with real-time feeds, multi-tenant isolation, and production-like failure modes. It also emphasizes contract testing between related services and resilience to downstream faults, all common in large tech ecosystems.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency testing under load with real-world patterns
- Per-tenant data isolation and data-ownership guarantees
- Consumer-driven contracts between interdependent APIs
- Resilience testing for downstream systems (brokers, queues) and retry/backoff strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// k6 basic latency test example
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const res = http.get('https://api.example.com/feed?tenant=tenantA&limit=50');
  check(res, { 'status 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you automate contract updates as API evolve, and what alerts would you set for contract violations?
- How would you detect potential data leakage across tenants during load testing and what safeguards would you implement?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle focusing on real-time WebSocket delivery and per-tenant isolation within shared infra, complementing REST-centric tests.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation in shared infra
- Idempotent REST endpoints with a WebSocket delivery path
- Backpressure, reconnect/backoff, and end-to-end latency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal WebSocket client test for in-order sequence_id
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example/notifications?tenant=abc');
let last = 0;
ws.onmessage = (e) => {
  const m = JSON.parse(e.data);
  if (m.sequence_id !== last + 1) throw new Error('out of order');
  last = m.sequence_id;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify deduplication if the same event arrives twice?
- What metrics would you collect to demonstrate backpressure handling and SLA adherence?

